WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.204 --> 00:00:03.171
- The paper airplane you
folded in middle school,

00:00:03.171 --> 00:00:04.739
horrible design.

00:00:04.739 --> 00:00:08.668
But, I've thrown a
paper airplane 214 feet.

00:00:08.668 --> 00:00:09.501
I'm John Collins.

00:00:09.501 --> 00:00:10.749
I am the paper airplane guy.

00:00:10.749 --> 00:00:13.952
World record holder for
paper aircraft distance.

00:00:13.952 --> 00:00:16.619
(playful music)

00:00:18.257 --> 00:00:20.036
The year we got the record was 2012.

00:00:20.036 --> 00:00:21.563
It was, you know, one of the great

00:00:21.563 --> 00:00:23.479
days in my life, actually.

00:00:23.479 --> 00:00:25.016
You know, I look back at my child being born

00:00:25.016 --> 00:00:25.849
and the world record.

00:00:25.849 --> 00:00:28.058
Those are great moments for me.

00:00:28.058 --> 00:00:29.488
I named the world record
plane after my wife.

00:00:29.488 --> 00:00:31.963
The name of the design is Susanne.

00:00:31.963 --> 00:00:33.017
This is how you fold Susanne.

00:00:33.017 --> 00:00:35.446
You start with the short
side of the paper out.

00:00:35.446 --> 00:00:38.279
(quickly talking)

00:00:40.886 --> 00:00:43.303
It's just eight simple folds.

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:48.187
There are a lot of different
kinds of folding techniques

00:00:48.187 --> 00:00:51.024
and a lot of different
kinds of paper airplanes.

00:00:51.024 --> 00:00:53.213
There's planes that boomerang back.

00:00:53.213 --> 00:00:55.451
There are planes that are
made to fly straight very far.

00:00:55.451 --> 00:00:56.505
There are planes that are made to stay

00:00:56.505 --> 00:00:59.296
in the air for a long time.

00:00:59.296 --> 00:01:02.103
Every great paper airplane,
distance or time aloft,

00:01:02.103 --> 00:01:04.274
has very accurate folding.

00:01:04.274 --> 00:01:06.561
Most people want to know how
I got into paper airplanes,

00:01:06.561 --> 00:01:08.341
and the reality is I
never got out of them.

00:01:08.341 --> 00:01:09.306
I am curious by nature.

00:01:09.306 --> 00:01:10.964
I love math and science.

00:01:10.964 --> 00:01:12.877
The cool thing about paper
airplanes is it's a way

00:01:12.877 --> 00:01:15.719
to actually discover
something real about flight.

00:01:15.719 --> 00:01:17.743
What most people don't
know is that we don't

00:01:17.743 --> 00:01:20.870
know the exact cause and effect for lift.

00:01:20.870 --> 00:01:22.353
Every little paper airplane throw

00:01:22.353 --> 00:01:24.038
is like a little science experiment.

00:01:24.038 --> 00:01:25.889
Here's the laboratory,
right here in your hand

00:01:25.889 --> 00:01:29.257
that you could use to do real science.

00:01:29.257 --> 00:01:31.755
Teachers kind of don't like
kids to do paper airplanes.

00:01:31.755 --> 00:01:34.467
It has this bad boy kinda
renegade reputation.

00:01:34.467 --> 00:01:36.437
If they could discover
that paper airplanes

00:01:36.437 --> 00:01:37.472
actually are science.

00:01:37.472 --> 00:01:39.209
That they're doing science
and having fun doing it.

00:01:39.209 --> 00:01:41.196
I think that's an
incredible breakthrough.

00:01:41.196 --> 00:01:45.363
Paper airplane throwing
class, yes (chuckles).

00:01:48.298 --> 00:01:50.965
(playful music)

